Given the constraint, let me go with a robust nudge strategy:

```ts
export function focusOn(camera: ArcRotateCamera, point: { x: number; y: number; z: number }): void {
    camera.target.x = point.x;
    camera.target.y = point.y;
    camera.target.z = point.z;

    // Force immediate view recompute by writing a changed value
    // then restoring it. Try alpha, then beta, then radius.
    const a = camera.alpha;
    camera.alpha = a + 1;
    if (camera.alpha !== a + 1) {
        camera.alpha = a;
        const b = camera.beta;
        camera.beta = b + 1;
        if (camera.beta !== b + 1) {
            camera.beta = b;
            const r = camera.radius;
            camera.radius = r + 1;
            camera.radius = r;
        } else {
            camera.beta = b;
        }
    } else {
        camera.alpha = a;
    }
}
```

Wait, but what if alpha is at the LOWER limit? Then `a + 1` is fine (it's above the lower limit), so it works. The problem is only when `a + 1` gets clamped, which happens when alpha is at the UPPER limit.

What if alpha is at the upper limit? `a + 1` gets clamped to `a` (upper limit). So `camera.alpha !== a + 1` is true (camera.alpha is a, not a+1). Then we reset alpha to a (same value, no trigger) and try beta.

What if beta is also at its upper limit? Same thing, we try radius.

What if radius is at its upper limit? `r + 1` gets clamped to `r`. Then `camera.radius = r` is the same value. No trigger. The target change won't show.

