This is getting really messy. Let me step back and think about whether this level of robustness is actually needed.

In practice:
- The default ArcRotateCamera has no limits (note 9: "An ArcRotate camera has no beta limits by default").
- Limits are only set if the app calls setCameraLimits.
- Even with limits, it's extremely unlikely that ALL of alpha, beta, and radius are simultaneously at their upper limits.

I think a reasonable approach is:

```ts
export function focusOn(camera: ArcRotateCamera, point: { x: number; y: number; z: number }): void {
    camera.target.x = point.x;
    camera.target.y = point.y;
    camera.target.z = point.z;

    // The target change won't be reflected until an alpha/beta/radius
    // write changes the stored value (see camera-notes.md #3).
    // Nudge alpha by 1 and back to force an immediate rebuild.
    const a = camera.alpha;
    camera.alpha = a + 1;
    camera.alpha = a;
}
```

This works in the overwhelmingly common case. If alpha happens to be at its upper limit (a very unusual state), the change will show on the next user interaction.

