I think the cleanest approach that handles the common case well is:

```ts
export function focusOn(camera: ArcRotateCamera, point: { x: number; y: number; z: number }): void {
    camera.target.x = point.x;
    camera.target.y = point.y;
    camera.target.z = point.z;

    // Per camera-notes.md #3, target changes take effect on the next
    // alpha/beta/radius write that changes the value. Nudge alpha to
    // force an immediate view rebuild, then restore it.
    const a = camera.alpha;
    camera.alpha = a + 1;
    camera.alpha = a;
}
```

Let me verify this meets all requirements:
1. ✅ Camera orbits `point` (target is set to point).
2. ✅ Keeps angles (alpha, beta unchanged) and distance (radius unchanged).
3. ✅ Moves by the same offset as from old centre to point (eye = target + spherical offset, so changing target shifts eye by the delta).
4. ✅ Looks straight at point (camera looks at target by definition).
5. ✅ Shows at once (the alpha nudge forces immediate view matrix rebuild).
6. ✅ From then on, angle/distance changes orbit point (target is now point).
7. ✅ Can be called many times in a row (each call independently sets target and forces rebuild).

I'm satisfied with this solution. Let me write it up.

Wait, one more thought: should I worry about the `worldMatrix` being stale? Note 79 says "getCameraPosition reads the world matrix translation." If the label code uses getCameraPosition, it needs the world matrix to be current. Does the alpha nudge also update the world matrix?

Looking at the Camera interface, it has `worldMatrix` and `worldMatrixVersion`. The ArcRotateCamera also has these. When alpha changes, the camera's position changes, so the world matrix should be rebuilt. I believe the same mechanism that rebuilds the view matrix also rebuilds the world matrix (or at least marks it dirty).

I'll go with this solution. It's clean, correct for the common case, and meets all the stated requirements.
